00:00.000 --> 00:06.180
Hello, I would like to talk today about least squares in a rather informal way and introduce

00:06.180 --> 00:11.940
you to a tool that is very handy for solving a large set of problems, especially state

00:11.940 --> 00:15.540
estimation problems, and that's what we will be using it for here.

00:15.540 --> 00:20.100
So just as a reminder, we start out with that because we want to use it as a tool to address

00:20.100 --> 00:24.820
the simultaneous localization problem, and we are especially looking here into one variant

00:24.820 --> 00:29.060
of the simultaneous localization mapping problem, which is a graph-based approach.

00:29.120 --> 00:36.300
So where we use a graph consisting of observations and controls and try to treat this as an optimization

00:36.300 --> 00:42.300
problem, and for that we will use the tool of least squares, so graph-based SLAM is nothing

00:42.300 --> 00:45.240
else than a least squares approach to SLAM.

00:45.240 --> 00:50.540
And in order to understand how that works, I would like to introduce you today into the

00:50.540 --> 00:51.880
least squares problem.

00:51.880 --> 00:54.420
So what is least squares for?

00:54.420 --> 00:59.540
It is a general approach for computing a solution of an overdetermined system, and

00:59.540 --> 01:07.160
it does that in a way that the small mistakes that we do everywhere is minimized, and we

01:07.160 --> 01:11.100
are minimizing the squared errors or the sum of the squared errors.

01:11.100 --> 01:13.980
So let's take a very concrete example first.

01:13.980 --> 01:15.920
What can be an overdetermined system?

01:15.920 --> 01:20.140
So for example, we have a set of parameters that we want to estimate, and we have a set

01:20.140 --> 01:24.440
of observations that we get which tell us something about those parameters.

01:24.440 --> 01:28.340
And then it is often the case that we have actually a large number of those observations

01:28.340 --> 01:35.300
because we want to estimate those parameters as good as possible, so we have more observations

01:35.300 --> 01:40.260
than parameters or more information in those observations than our parameters.

01:40.260 --> 01:44.420
And then, given that we have always noise in our observations, we will not find a perfect

01:44.420 --> 01:45.420
solution.

01:45.420 --> 01:52.300
We will not find the set of parameters that brings all the whole system to zero mistakes.

01:52.300 --> 01:56.140
So there will always be small noise values remaining.

01:56.140 --> 02:01.580
And we're trying to find parameters that minimize the sum of those squared errors.

02:01.580 --> 02:05.080
So overall, we say we have more equations than unknowns.

02:05.080 --> 02:09.460
Unknowns are our parameters, equations are the things that, at least in the way we use

02:09.460 --> 02:13.540
least squares, stem from our observations.

02:13.540 --> 02:19.100
And we want to find the unknowns or parameters so that the sum of the squared errors is minimized.

02:19.100 --> 02:20.580
And that's the standard approach.

02:20.580 --> 02:24.680
It is out there since more than 200 years.

02:24.680 --> 02:30.260
It's very frequently used in a lot of engineering problems, and we often use it here in order

02:30.260 --> 02:34.220
to estimate model parameters given a set of observations.

02:34.220 --> 02:41.260
So in the context of the SLAM problem, our observations can be sensor observations like

02:41.260 --> 02:48.280
